s  all right  so  let's move further forwards into  the uncertain fog of the future and  think about what we might do  as we go forwards much further now  everything i'm about to say for the rest  of the talk is not a commitment it's  just a bunch of experiments that we're  doing that we are trying to get your  feedback about and see if this is of  interest to you  and this is going to be not actually  even about blazer this is going to be  about net on web assembly in general and  specifically webassembly on the server  and to understand how that works and why  it's interesting we need to start by  taking a quick step back to make sure  everyone really understands what  webassembly itself actually is  so webassembly has been around for many  years like seven years or something by  now but what actually is it well it's a  by-code format it's a way of  representing instructions that are  independent of any particular cpu  architecture or or operating system and  it is designed for running your code in  a sandbox it was originally designed for  running inside a browser where you will  be running hostile code so the sandbox  needs to be incredibly rigorous and  bulletproof and to make sure it's that  rigorous the only thing that webassembly  bytecode can do by default is perform  operations on its own memory that's it  it just changes numbers in its own  memory space  which is kind of pointless if there's no  way of interacting with the outside  world so the other main thing that it  has is the ability to import and export  functions so the host that is running  your code can import functions into the  webassembly which it can then call and  webassembly can export functions that  the host can call  okay so that's fine  but if we want to use this for  server-side programming the code is  going to have to interact with the  operating system in some way  ideally through some functions that we  import but what would those function  imports be wouldn't it be nice if there  