y vulnerability well even in that  case the sandbox is going to protect the  host and it allows the host to control  things like what the code can do can it  access the disk can it access the  network but even beyond that it allows  the host to constrain things like how  many cpu instructions is this call  allowed to take before i say it's using  too much cpu resources how much memory  is it allowed to allocate so the host  can very rigorously defend itself  against whatever code it's running and  then as well as that it also simplifies  things in terms of the development  experience so the host doesn't need a  custom build tool chain it can just  accept a single wasm file that's been  built by any existing wasm tool chain  and then on the other side of the  equation for the application developer  it means that you can write your code in  any programming language at all as long  as it compiles to webassembly which is  most of them now and it means that when  you are deploying your application you  don't have to care what operating system  it's got being run on or even what cpu  architecture because the same binary is  going to work everywhere so even if your  development machine has got an arm  processor and the host is x64 or vice  versa you don't have to care about that  the same binary will run everywhere and  because of the nature of webassembly  which has always been built designed for  speed you should be able to get  something approximating native speed not  exactly but close enough that hopefully  it won't matter  okay so let's think about net now in  this environment and what uh how we  would actually use that in fact actually  before that just i just want to add a  note so you might look at this and say  hey that's actually kind of similar to  some of the same benefits that you get  with containerization like docker or  similar and it's true there are some  similarities in fact some people have  been saying that wazi could be the next  big evolution in clo